  "_licenseNote": "Optional field since v5.6.0: \"licenseType\" states how the matched product is LICENSED, so an asset report does not have to re-derive it per device. Values are either an SPDX license identifier (open source: MIT, Apache-2.0, MPL-2.0, GPL-2.0-only, GPL-3.0-or-later, LGPL-2.1-only, AGPL-3.0-only, PSF-2.0, MS-PL, MS-RL, PHP-3.01, curl, ...) or one of exactly three descriptors: \"Proprietary\" (closed source, a purchased license is required for the use in question), \"Freeware\" (closed source, free of charge - this includes hardware/OS-bundled vendor tools such as the Dell, Intel, Lenovo, Logitech, Epson, NVIDIA, APC and NETGEAR companions) and \"Freeware (non-commercial)\" (free only for private, non-commercial use - a business fleet needs a paid license, e.g. IrfanView). The field describes the SOFTWARE license, never the purchase model of one installation: whether a specific machine holds a Retail, OEM or Volume key and whether it is activated comes from the device (SoftwareLicensingProduct LicenseStatus/ProductKeyChannel, see license-table.json) and seats/expiry/purchase data comes from the asset-management system. Omit the field when there is no verifiable statement - never guess. \"kind\" (driver/firmware) rows carry no licenseType on purpose: they are exclusion markers without a product identity. Evidence and regeneration: the values were read from the winget community manifests (microsoft/winget-pkgs, MIT license) with \"winget show --id <id> --exact --locale en-US\" and, where winget has no package, from the project's own LICENSE statement; \"node tools/gen-license-meta.js --refresh\" repeats that lookup and reports drift. Changing only this file needs no release (running servers re-download it).",
